\begin{abstract}
Environmental conditions play an important role in changing the nature of wireless communication channels, making wireless communication channels highly unpredictable. Environmental conditions can also vary significantly depending on the audio or video signal being transmitted. The use of fixed modulation techniques tends to result in increased bit error rate (BER) due to the lack of optimal use of the available bandwidth in high SNR environments. Because fixed modulation techniques tend to result in lower reliability, adaptive modulation techniques have been created to help provide some level of reliability by automatically switching the modulation technique used to transmit information based upon whether the channel is operating at a low SNR or high SNR environment. Adaptive modulation techniques can automatically select the proper modulation technique using a context-aware system to match the modulation technique to the SNR of the channel. A context-aware adaptive modulation system will automatically switch between BPSK, QPSK or 16 QAM modulations based upon the current channel conditions changing to maximize efficiency.Using MATLAB, we will develop and simulate a prototype of the proposed context-aware adaptive modulation system for performance evaluation; specifically, we will compare its Bit Error Rate performance to that of conventional systems. To accomplish this, we will use simulation results to show that the context-aware adaptive modulation system makes fewer mistakes than an alternative type of modulation system and provides greater throughput than fixed and adaptive modulation channels. Ultimately, the context-aware adaptive modulation system will allow for improved system efficiency.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Adaptive Modulation Techniques, Software Defined Radio Systems, Context-Aware Wireless Communication, SNR Estimation, Bit Error Rate Analysis, Spectral Efficiency Optimization, MATLAB-Based Simulation, 5G/6G Networks.

\end{IEEEkeywords}


\section{Introduction}


The increasing need for high-speed data communication with reliable links and high spectral efficiency are due to improvements in technology for wireless communications. The wireless telecommunications system must adapt to a rapidly changing and highly dynamic environment in which it operates due to variations in the communication channel that are a result of many variables (such as noise, hopping points, mobility of users, other users in the same frequency band, and environmental factors) that can affect the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), which is the main measurement for determining how well an operator can extract useful information at the receiver. When the SNR has significant variation, it becomes difficult to develop the best strategy for transmitting and receiving the information.

Historically, communication systems used fixed modulation types; that is, they did not change as the channel changed (eg. Poor weather or interference). Fixed modulation methods provide lower levels of reliability compared to adaptive methods, where you can have both high and low frequencies; however, you have a limit on your SNR that allows you to pass low or high frequencies based on channel conditions. If your SNR is low, then you will have high Bit Error Rates (BER) when you use high-frequency modulation; therefore, you will not have reliable communications. Conversely, if you are in a high-SNR situation, you can use low-frequency modulation which will lower the data rate and will waste your precious bandwidth over the communication link. It is this trade-off that creates a need for advanced, intelligent, dynamic communication systems that can adapt their modulation methods based on changes in channel conditions to maintain reliable and high-rate communications.
An excellent way to enhance your communication over an underperforming communication link is through the intelligent use of adaptive modulation. When you use adaptive modulation techniques, you adapt your modulation techniques to suit the SNR to give you your optimum modulation scheme. The better your SNR, the higher the frequency of the modulation scheme you use to achieve your optimum modulation.Low-order modulation techniques like BPSK are more reliable than higher-order techniques like QPSK or 16-QAM due to their ability to withstand noise; therefore they will be used when a low-quality channel is present. Conversely, high-order modulations like QPSK and 16-QAM have the highest data rates, making them ideal selections when using high-quality channels.

Adaptive Modulation defines a balance point between two independent characteristics within a channel. 

Recent developments in Software Defined Radio (SDR) technology have helped to redefine the concept of adaptive communications. SDR enables the modification of various communication parameters (modulation schemes, coding rates, transmission power) via software control and dynamic response.

The combination of intelligent decision-making algorithms with programmable communication parameters allows an Adaptive Communications System to dynamically monitor and respond to changes in channel conditions by adjusting the communication protocol being used. Adaptive Communications has become a key element for many of today's advanced wireless technologies including next generation mobile communications and Internet of Things (IoT) applications.
Apart from this, there is improvement based upon greater spectral efficiency as well. Spectral efficiency refers to the best utilisation of bandwidth for transmitting data. Bandwidth allocation in many newer wireless communications has been regulated strictly because of the limited resources available in the frequency band. Consider multiple modulation schemes – higher order Modulation over higher SNR and lower order modulation over lower SNR – throughout the overall system, which will have a corresponding improvement to performance of the overall system and not require increased complexity to system design.

This work contains the design and implementation of Context Aware SNR Based Adaptive Modulation Systems simulated via MATLAB. The system designed can measure Channel SNR and select of an appropriate adaptive modulation scheme (BPSK, QPSK or 16-QAM) based upon the measured Channel SNR to evaluate the overall performance of the proposed system using BER analysis with the objective of analytically demonstrating a relationship between SNR, Modulation Order and BER to show how the use of an Adaptive Modulation Scheme can provide for greater efficiencies than conventional fixed modulation techniques resulting in a more intelligent and capable means by which to provide wireless communication that has an improved BER in Low/High SNR Values with support for high data rate at high SNR values.In addition to that, improvement is also very present due to better spectral efficiency. Spectral efficiency means that the user is using the bandwidth that they have available to them. Because of the limitations that exist in the frequency band, the bandwidth usage in a large percentage of advanced wireless communications is being regulated at a high level by the appropriate governing authorities.

Different modulation methods exist when you consider operating at higher SNR with higher-order modulation methods and lower-order modulation methods at lower SNR. Depending on the specific combination of modulation methods used, the operation of the system can offer improved performance when compared to a similar system using other modulation options. These improvements will not require higher complexity in terms of the overall system's design and will still have a positive impact on the performance of the overall wireless communication system's operation.

This document outlines the design and development of contextually aware SNR adaptable modulation systems that were created with MATLAB.

\section{Literature Survey}

Several researchers have explored the idea of using adaptive modulation to increase both ability to use spectral resources (e.g., faster speeds) and reliability (e.g., by using different levels of modulation based upon weather changes). These investigations have been focused largely on the consideration of the instantaneous channel state (CSI) in order to create an improved performance wireless system in a fading environment. The authors Goldsmith and Chua, in reference to [1], proposed the idea of using variable bit rate (i.e., adaptive) transmission in fading channels, and demonstrated through both theoretical and simulation work that this technique would increase the ability to use spectral resources.

Researchers in [2] have continued to conduct research on AQAM and further evaluate its use in determining the performance of wireless communications systems employing adaptive modulation schemes based on OFDM. The utilization of an adaptive modulation scheme in a wireless communications system can enable lower bit error rates (BER) while optimizing the maximum data rate. Wireless communications systems typically use BPSK and QPSK in low SNR environments due to their high immunity to noise. When the SNR of a channel is increased and becomes stable, higher-order modulation schemes are available to provide increased data rates. Examples of these higher-order modulation schemes are 16-QAM and 64-QAM.

Adaptive Communication Techniques allow you to be flexible and able to adapt to the use of Software Defined Radio (SDR). They also enable you to provide an adaptable communication system that can adapt to changing conditions through either the use of cognitive radio technology as defined by Mitola (2002) or through intelligent spectrum sensing and adaptability of parameters. By implementing context aware decision algorithms on an SDR platform, the performance and efficiency of an SDR platform may also be enhanced by the monitoring and adjustment of the system in relation to its environment. Performance studies (original study [4]) support this assertion. In addition, new research in adaptive modulation techniques that allow for increased data rates with minimal latency in the next generation (5G+) wireless networks have emerged from [5], which focus upon the ability to adapt to changing conditions and as a result improve the QoS through the use of link adaptation techniques based upon the measured SNR. Finally, [6] demonstrates through simulations that adaptive modulation provides improved BER in time varying channels compared with conventional modulation.




\section{Objective}

The major objectives of this research are as follows:

\begin{itemize}

\item The objectives of this study include the following:

• To develop and implement an adaptive modulation framework using software-defined radio concepts to support efficient and flexible wireless communication.


\item To investigate the performance limitations of traditional non-context-aware software-defined radio-based communication systems with fixed transmission parameters under dynamic wireless communication channels.

\item To investigate how modulation order, transmission power, and bandwidth affect system performance metrics such as BER, throughput, spectral efficiency, latency, and energy efficiency.

\item To analyze the influence of modulation order, transmission power, and bandwidth on key system performance indicators such as BER, throughput, spectral efficiency, latency, and energy efficiency.

\item To design and simulate an SNR-based adaptive decision-making algorithm to choose the appropriate modulation schemes such as BPSK, QPSK, and 16-QAM.

\item To develop a quantitative relationship between channel variability (SNR changes) and the selection of the modulation order for optimum system performance.

\item To show the effectiveness of context awareness in reducing the number of retransmissions, increasing the throughput, and improving the efficiency of the overall communication process.


\section{Materials and Methodology}

The proposed Adaptive Modulation based Software Defined Radio (SDR) system is designed using mathematical modeling, channel simulation, and a threshold based adaptive control mechanism. This section explains the system model, channel model, adaptive modulation algorithm, and the performance evaluation metrics used in the proposed system.

\subsection{System Model}

The communication system follows a digital baseband transmission model consisting of a data source, adaptive modulator, wireless channel, demodulator, and performance analyzer. Random binary sequences are generated and mapped to modulation symbols based on the selected modulation scheme.

The transmitted signal can be expressed as

\begin{equation}
s(t) = A \cos(2\pi f_c t + \phi)
\end{equation}

where $A$ denotes the amplitude of the signal, $f_c$ represents the carrier frequency, and $\phi$ indicates the phase of the carrier.

\subsection{Signal-to-Noise Ratio}

Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) is a key parameter used to describe the quality of a communication channel. It is defined as the ratio between the signal power and the noise power present in the system.

\begin{equation}
\text{SNR} = \frac{P_{signal}}{P_{noise}}
\end{equation}

where $P_{signal}$ represents the power of the transmitted signal and $P_{noise}$ denotes the noise power introduced by the channel.

\subsection{Bit Error Rate}

Bit Error Rate (BER) is a performance metric used to measure the reliability of a digital communication system. It indicates the proportion of incorrectly received bits compared to the total number of transmitted bits.

\begin{equation}
\text{BER} = \frac{N_{error}}{N_{total}}
\end{equation}

where $N_{error}$ is the number of bits received in error and $N_{total}$ represents the total number of transmitted bits.

For BPSK modulation over an AWGN channel, the theoretical BER can be expressed as

\begin{equation}
BER_{BPSK} = Q\left(\sqrt{\frac{2E_b}{N_0}}\right)
\end{equation}

where $E_b$ denotes the energy per bit and $N_0$ represents the noise power spectral density.

\subsection{Channel Model}

In this study, the communication channel is modeled as an Additive White Gaussian Noise (AWGN) channel. Under this model, the received signal is represented as

\begin{equation}
r(t) = s(t) + n(t)
\end{equation}

where $s(t)$ denotes the transmitted signal and $n(t)$ represents the Gaussian noise component with zero mean and variance $\sigma^{2}$.

\subsection{Adaptive Modulation Algorithm}

The adaptive modulation algorithm dynamically selects the modulation scheme according to the estimated SNR value. Let $\gamma$ represent the estimated SNR value. Two threshold levels $\gamma_1$ and $\gamma_2$ are defined to control the switching mechanism.

\begin{equation}
M =
\begin{cases}
\text{BPSK}, & \gamma < \gamma_1 \\
\text{QPSK}, & \gamma_1 \leq \gamma < \gamma_2 \\
\text{16-QAM}, & \gamma \geq \gamma_2
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

This adaptive mechanism ensures reliable communication under poor channel conditions and improves spectral efficiency when the channel quality is high.

\subsection{Performance Metrics}

The efficiency of the proposed communication system is analyzed using several evaluation parameters, including Bit Error Rate (BER), spectral efficiency, and system throughput.

Spectral efficiency indicates how efficiently the available bandwidth is utilized for data transmission and is expressed as

\begin{equation}
\eta = \log_2(M)
\end{equation}

where $M$ denotes the modulation order.

The throughput of the system can be calculated as

\begin{equation}
\text{Throughput} = R(1 - BER)
\end{equation}

where $R$ represents the raw data transmission rate of the communication system.

% ------------------------------------------------------------------

\section{Proposed System}
A software-defined radio (SDR) framework is used to implement a smart and adaptive modulation scheme for the suggested system. Unlike standard communication systems, which use fixed modulation techniques, the suggested system uses an adaptive modulation scheme depending on real-time channel conditions. By using an adaptive technique to modulate based on real-time conditions, the tapered modulated-communication system increases the reliability of communications through lower quality channels and improves communication efficiency and data-rate through higher quality channels.

\subsection{Architecture}
The overall structure of the proposed adaptive SDR system is shown in Fig.~1. The process starts with a random binary data generator that creates sequences of bits representing the information to be transmitted. These generated bits are then sent to an adaptive modulation controller, which selects a suitable modulation scheme according to the current channel conditions.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{word.png}
\caption{System Architecture of the Proposed Adaptive SDR System}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

The SNR estimation component tracks the SNR of wireless channels as they change with the use of information from multiple estimation methods in conjunction with measured values to create a dynamic network SNR. Once the estimated SNR value is established, along with the use of preset thresholds, the decision model will determine which of the possible modulation methods (BPSK, QPSK, or 16-QAM) have the greatest potential for successful operation depending on the range of SNR values (higher or lower than the configured threshold) and the measurement for the current SNR.

If the measured SNR is lower than the configured SNR threshold, the decision-making process will select the lowest modulation scheme (BPSK) to ensure that any transmitted data will be sent without error. Conversely, if the current SNR eventually rises above the configured SNR threshold, the decision module will switch from the BPSK modulation scheme to a higher modulation method (either QPSK or 16-QAM). Each of these decisions will occur in real-time based on the current SNR measurement.

The SDR transmitter sends the output of the generated signal modulate to pass it through a wireless communication link as a modulated signal. During the transmission of the signal over the wireless communication channel, the transmitted signal may experience distortion due to the noise present in that channel. The SDR receiver receives the received signal from the transmitter, demodulates it to recover its original binary data and compares it (the received data) to the transmitted data, thereby allowing the calculation of a Bit Error Rate (BER) for the system being tested. In this evaluation, the BER, throughput, and spectral efficiency will be used as KPIs to measure how successful the designed solution was.

\subsection{Workflow}

The operational workflow of the proposed adaptive modulation based SDR system is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:workflow}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{work23.png}
\caption{Workflow of Adaptive Modulation based SDR System}
\label{fig:workflow}
\end{figure}

The first step in the workflow of creating random binary data is to ensure there will be some data made available to transit through a telecommunications (telecom) system after the binary data has been created, it may then be modulated using the modulation technique that was pre-selected. The modulated signal will travel through the wireless medium from the transmitter/transponder to the receiver and will be subject to degradation from noise and other channel distortions along the way.

Upon receiving the modulated signal, the receiving unit will estimate the Signal to Noise Ratio (SNR) of the channel to ascertain how well the communication channel processed the signal. The SNR value will then be compared to a predetermined threshold level, which will allow the receiving unit to detect and determine the best modulation scheme to use based on the current conditions of the channel.

The process of Demodulating received information from received signal to original information is needed to obtain the original data from the received signal, as well as in order to calculate the bit error rate (BER). In order to calculate BER, you need to count the number of erroneous bits in the received as compared to the number of transmitted bits. By analyzing system performance with respect to BER, throughput, spectral efficiency, etc., the ability of adaptive modulation to be effective at communicating over a dynamic wireless channel can be assessed. The information gathered from the analysis can be used to evaluate the adaptability and reliability of the adaptive modulation framework and how well it will perform when the channel conditions vary. Therefore, the implementation of the adaptive modulation framework improves the reliability of communication and increases the efficiency with which bandwidth is utilized.

\section{Results and Discussion}

The proposed adaptive modulation SDR system is analyzed under an Additive White Gaussian Noise (AWGN) channel environment. In the simulation study, the Bit Error Rate (BER) performance of different modulation techniques including BPSK, QPSK, 16-QAM, and the adaptive modulation scheme is examined across a Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) range of 0 dB to 20 dB.

\subsection{BER vs SNR Performance}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{result.jpeg}
\caption{BER versus SNR performance for fixed and adaptive modulation}
\label{fig:ber}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:ber} Below is the comparison of Bit Error Rate (BER) among many types of modulation as a function of SNR. Of the three modulations (BPSK, QPSK, 16-QAM), BPSK performs best with respect to BER at low SNR (less than 5 dB). Although BPSK provides a better BER, it also has a lower expected data rate than that of QPSK and 16-QAM; both have higher data rates than BPSK, but require high SNR for reliable operations.

QPSK has a better BER than BPSK, but worse data rate than BPSK; it does, however, provide a higher spectral efficiency. 16-QAM must operate between SNRs above 16 dB in order to provide a reasonable BER as compared to QPSK due to the shorter distances separating constellation points.

To optimize both throughput (data rate) and spectral efficiency and enhance the operation of the modulating system, an adaptive system was created to dynamically switch between BPSK, QPSK, and 16-QAM protocols according to channel performance. The adaptive system uses BPSK for reliable communication with low SNR (less than 10 dB), then switches to QPSK and/or 16-QAM at SNRs greater than 10 dB to achieve high spectral efficiency and throughput.

Based upon the results of the simulation above, the adaptive modulating technique will produce better overall results by providing a low BER for poor channels while providing a high throughput for good channels.

\section{System Features Evaluation}

\subsection{Innovation of the Proposed Adaptive Framework}
The proposed system presents an adaptive modulation framework driven by SNR in a software-defined radio (SDR) environment. Unlike traditional mobile communication systems that use fixed modulation methods, this system allows for real-time coordination of the modulation technique (such as BPSK, QPSK, and 16-QAM) based on the current state of the wireless channel. This adaptive feature ensures that the system operates reliably regardless of how much variation exists in the wireless environment.

To implement these features, the proposed framework employs an SNR-driven closed-loop channel-aware decision mechanism that continuously tracks and estimates the SNR. Based on the estimated SNR, the system will which modulation order to transmit based on current channel conditions. This process enables transmitters to achieve the best possible balance between reliable communications and efficient use of spectrum. The system uses mid-level design to enable comparison of different modulation schemes across multiple trials in the same environment with respect to throughput (TP) and spectral efficiency (SE).
The spectral efficiency of the system varies depending on the modulation order being used and can be mathematically expressed as

\begin{equation}
\eta = \log_{2}(M)
\end{equation}

where $M$ represents the modulation order. When higher modulation orders are applied under favorable channel conditions, the system is able to utilize the available bandwidth more efficiently and achieve higher data transmission rates.

In order to represent realistic wireless environments, an Additive White Gaussian Noise (AWGN) based channel model with varying SNR levels is incorporated in the system simulation.

The adaptive switching between modulation schemes is controlled using optimized SNR threshold values \(\gamma_1\) and \(\gamma_2\). When the SNR is low, the system selects BPSK to maintain reliable communication. For moderate SNR conditions, QPSK is chosen, while 16-QAM is utilized when the channel quality is sufficiently high. This threshold-based switching strategy minimizes bit errors and avoids unnecessary retransmissions.

Furthermore, the proposed framework enhances data transmission performance by improving the achievable throughput. The system throughput can be represented as

\begin{equation}
\text{Throughput} = R(1 - BER)
\end{equation}

where $R$ denotes the data transmission rate and $BER$ represents the bit error rate. By dynamically selecting an appropriate modulation scheme according to the channel condition, the system is able to achieve better throughput compared with conventional fixed modulation approaches.



\subsection{System-Level Impact Assessment}

The use of adaptive modulation in an SDR architecture greatly enhances the reliability and stability of wireless communications operating in changing SNR environments. Continuous channel estimation enables the communication system to achieve lower bit error rates even in harsh environmental conditions. Adaptability of the communication system allows for relative channel fluctuation, thus sustaining performance levels that are more consistent. This structuring of communication enhances the efficiency of the overall communication and allows more efficient data transfers in dynamic wireless environments.

 The dynamic nature of the system provides for greater spectral efficiency and better usage of bandwidth by using the best modulation scheme as compared to a fixed modulation scheme. The dynamic selection mechanism increases throughput for improved channel conditions, providing for optimal data transfer.



