\noindent\textbf{Key Advantages Over Existing Methods –}
The proposed adaptive modulation framework provides numerous enhancements over conventional Software Defined Radio (SDR) systems. The capability of dynamically adapting the channel allows the SDR system to constantly monitor the Signal to Noise Ratio (SNR) and automatically select the most appropriate modulation scheme that will provide reliable communications despite changing channel conditions. Adaptive switching among modulation schemes such as BPSK, QPSK, and 16-QAM improves the spectral efficiency of the SDR-based communication system and enhances throughput as the channel conditions become better. An SNR-based threshold mechanism is used to determine the most suitable modulation scheme during unfavorable channel conditions, which helps in reducing the Bit Error Rate (BER).

Furthermore, the use of adaptive modulation minimizes unnecessary retransmissions and reduces power consumption, thereby improving the overall energy efficiency of the system. The SDR-based architecture also offers a flexible platform that can easily support integration with emerging wireless technologies, including fifth-generation (5G) networks, Internet of Things (IoT) applications, and cognitive radio systems.

\section{Conclusion}

This study presents an adaptive modulation software-defined radio (SDR) system that incorporates real-time SNR measurements into its design and assessment methodology. The communication system employs modulation switching among BPSK, QPSK and 16-QAM based on current channel conditions. The SDR system offers adaptive capabilities, allowing it to maintain reliable communication performance across a variety of wireless networks. Through simulation analysis, the adaptive modulation method produces lower Bit Error Rates (BER) as the SNR decreases. 

As the quality of the channel continues to increase, the system's capacity to deliver increased throughput and optimal use of available bandwidth will also continue to increase. Unlike conventional fixed modulated SDR systems, the adaptive SDR model offers the ability to dynamically select an appropriate modulation scheme for the existing channel state to provide an appropriate balance between reliability and spectral efficiency.

By applying dynamic adaptation to the SDR architecture, stable performance is achieved regardless of the changing nature of wireless channel environments. The results also demonstrate that adding adaptive decision-making capabilities to an SDR architecture greatly increases systems performance by reducing retransmissions, minimizing energy consumption, and providing consistent quality of service (QoS).



